2026-09-03

[FINDING] Read-aloud test — worst offenders trip a reading-aloud parent
- surah-fatiha.html:194 "We ask Allah for the path of the good people He was pleased with — the prophets and the
  truthful. And we ask Him to keep us away from the two wrong paths: the path of people who knew
  the truth but turned away, and the path of people who got lost from it." Two em-dash/colon
  clauses stacked in one breath; a parent has to pre-read this before saying it aloud.
  Spoken rewrite: "We ask Allah for the path of good people — the prophets, the truthful ones.
  Not the path of people who knew the truth and turned away. Not the path of people who got lost."
  (Break into three short sentences a child can follow verse by verse.)
- surah-al-maun.html:90-91 "Here is the part that surprises everybody. The surah says 'woe to
  those who pray' — wait, is praying not a good thing? Read the next line and it becomes clear:
  these are people who are heedless about their prayer, and who pray to be seen." The aside
  ("wait, is praying not a good thing?") reads like a written rhetorical device, not something a
  parent naturally says aloud mid-sentence — it needs to become an actual question directed at the
  child, with a pause. Spoken rewrite: "The surah says something surprising: 'woe to those who
  pray.' [pause — ask: Wait, isn't praying a good thing?] Keep listening — it's about people who
  only pray so others will see them."
- surah-al-maun.html:69 "'Al-Maun' means the small helpful things — everyday bits and pieces
  people borrow from each other. A cup of sugar. A pen. A bucket. Little things that cost you
  almost nothing to share." This one actually speaks very well (short fragments, concrete nouns) —
  flagged as a positive model for the rewrites above.
- story-prophet-musa.html:74 "Long ago in Egypt there ruled a cruel king called Pharaoh. He
  thought he was a god, and he treated the Children of Israel very badly. One day he gave a
  terrible order: every baby boy born to them must be taken away." Fine aloud, but it's the
  opening paragraph and already carries three separate facts (setting, villain, threat) with no
  built-in pause point for a child reaction — add a beat after "he thought he was a god."
- Good news: most paragraphs across all five pages are short (2-3 sentences), present-tense,
  concrete, and built with a "Quick Question!" quiz card every 2-3 story beats — this genuinely
  gives natural pause-and-ask points (story-prophet-musa.html:100, story-prophet-nuh.html:100).
  This is the site's strongest read-aloud asset and should not be disturbed when editing prose.
- Session length: each page is one story/surah + intro + verse-by-verse + video + quiz + hadith +
  fun facts — roughly 400-700 spoken words of narrative content, comparable to a single bedtime
  story. Reasonable for one sitting. No single page is egregiously long; stopping point is clear
  (quiz results screen / "Back to Stories" nav).

[FINDING] Control test — the YouTube dependency, verified against actual markup
- grep across all 16 surah/story pages confirms 16 iframes at src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/<id>"
  (surah-al-falaq.html:208, surah-al-maun.html:238, surah-fatiha.html:234,
  story-prophet-musa.html:281, story-prophet-nuh.html:237, plus 11 more).
- Iframe attributes checked directly (surah-al-falaq.html:205-213, story-prophet-musa.html:278-286):
  plain youtube.com/embed domain (not youtube-nocookie.com), NO rel param, NO modestbranding, NO
  playsinline. allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope;
  picture-in-picture" and allowfullscreen are set.
- What that markup actually exposes a child to: end-screen suggested-video grid after playback
  (rel is unset, so YouTube shows related videos which can be from any channel, not just the
  same one); a full "Watch on YouTube" / channel-link escape hatch baked into the player chrome;
  ads (pre-roll/mid-roll, YouTube's choice, unrelated to content curation on this site); and,
  once tapped through to youtube.com on a phone, the entire YouTube recommendation surface,
  comments, and sign-in prompts — none of which the father chose or reviewed.
- Live-checked all 16 video IDs via YouTube's oEmbed endpoint right now: all 16 return 200 (none
  currently 404). This means the three previously-broken videos mentioned in the charter have
  since been fixed or swapped — but the underlying risk is structural, not a one-time bug: any of
  these 16 can go private, be deleted, get age-restricted, or get replaced by the uploader at any
  time, with zero warning to this site.
- Alternatives, ranked, with honest costs:
  1. **Drop video, lean on the existing ElevenLabs narration (RECOMMENDED).** The site already
     ships pre-generated ElevenLabs audio + a "Listen Mode" toggle for 4 of 5 stories checked
     (audio/prophet-adam, prophet-ibrahim, prophet-nuh, prophet-yusuf — audio-narration.js:321).
     Cost: build/extend narration for surah pages and for story-prophet-musa.html (audio folder
     confirms Musa currently has NO narration audio — a real gap). No new production cost beyond
     what's already proven to work; zero third-party risk; works offline; unchanged in five years.
     This is the only option that fully passes "if this changed tomorrow without us, would we
     notice?" — answer: nothing to change, because nothing depends on anyone else.
  2. **Father records his own narration/video.** Highest emotional value (his son's memory of his
     own recorded voice, not a stranger's) but highest production cost — time, equipment, one
     recording per surah/story, re-recording if content changes. Best long-term investment if he
     has the time; worth doing for a handful of favorites even without doing all 16.
  3. **Self-host video/audio files.** Full control, no ads/recommendations/escape hatch. Cost:
     needs the right to redistribute the specific videos (most YouTube kids'-Quran-recitation
     videos are not licensed for this), plus hosting/bandwidth. Only viable for content the site
     has explicit rights to, which likely rules out most of the current 16.
  4. **Switch to youtube-nocookie.com + rel=0 + modestbranding=1, keep YouTube.** Cheapest fix
     (edit 16 src attributes). Reduces cookie tracking and trims some chrome, but does NOT remove
     ads, does NOT remove the "Watch on YouTube" escape, and does NOT stop the video from being
     deleted/gone tomorrow. Treats a product problem as a technical one — a bandage, not a fix.
  5. **Keep YouTube as-is, add a periodic liveness check.** Catches breakage (like the 3 that
     already 404'd) but does nothing about content the father does not control while the video is
     still up — recommendations, ads, and the platform's choices remain. Worth doing as a safety
     net ONLY in combination with another option, never as the whole answer.
- Recommendation: adopt (1) now — it costs nothing new to build for 4 of 5 pages checked and just
  needs Musa's narration filled in — and treat (2) as the aspirational upgrade for the surahs and
  stories the father cares most about. Do not settle for (4) alone; it is the option most likely
  to be mistaken for "done" when the actual risk (an embed he does not own or control) is still
  live on every page.

[FINDING] Other third-party dependencies and what each risks for a child's session
- Google Fonts (index.html:9-11, surah-fatiha.html:9-11, and presumably all pages): a <link> to
  fonts.googleapis.com/fonts.gstatic.com on every page load. Leaks the visitor's IP and a
  fingerprint-able request pattern to Google on every page view; does not expose the child to any
  content, but it is a live network dependency — if Google changes/removes the font family or the
  CDN has an outage, page typography breaks. Low risk, easy self-host fix (download the 3 font
  families, serve locally) that also removes the dependency in one shot.
- GoatCounter analytics (index.html:201-203, script + noscript pixel): per commit history
  ("Keep GoatCounter analytics only on main page") this is intentionally scoped to index.html
  only, confirmed absent from surah-fatiha.html. Privacy-respecting, cookie-free analytics by
  design, and already scoped down to one page — low risk, no action needed.
- YouTube embed (see above) — the only dependency that puts unreviewed third-party content
  directly in front of the child, not just a network call.
- No other CDN scripts (no jsdelivr/cloudflare/unpkg) found in the pages checked — the JS
  (audio-narration.js, stories.js, translations.js, interactive-story.js, surah.js) is all local.

[FINDING] Product gaps for this exact use case, ranked by what this father and son would notice
1. Musa's story has no pre-recorded narration (audio/ folder has prophet-adam, prophet-ibrahim,
   prophet-nuh, prophet-yusuf but no prophet-musa) even though the page includes the same
   "Listen Mode" toggle pattern as the others — a parent who reaches for Listen Mode on Musa will
   hit a hole the other four stories don't have.
2. The YouTube dependency itself (detailed above) — the single highest-leverage fix for "I
   control what my son sees," and the one most likely to break again exactly as it did before.
3. No print/no-screen mode. A bedtime read-aloud tool built for a phone/tablet screen still asks
   the parent to hold a lit device at bedtime; a simple print-friendly or "text-only" view (verses
   + story text, no video/quiz chrome) would suit an actual bedtime routine better than the
   quiz-and-video web-page format built for daytime interactive use.
4. No "read together" progress or memory across sessions beyond the in-page quiz score — nothing
   marks which surahs/stories father and son have already done, so re-visits start cold with no
   sense of "we've done 6 of 16 together."
5. No explicit start of story-prophet-musa.html "Listen Mode" tie-in: the video is framed as a
   quiz-unlocked "bonus" (story-prophet-musa.html:271-273 "Bonus Video Unlocked!") which trains
   the child to expect a YouTube reward at the end of every story — reinforcing exactly the
   dependency flagged as the top control-test risk, and one the father may not have intended as a
   built-in incentive structure.

[DECISION] Recommend prioritizing: (a) fill in Musa's missing narration audio, (b) replace the
YouTube "bonus video unlock" pattern site-wide with existing/expanded ElevenLabs narration rather
than patching YouTube params, (c) self-host Google Fonts to remove the one remaining silent
network dependency. Scope call belongs to the owner; this is the CEO's ranked recommendation only.
